WEBVTT

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:09.760
Welcome to the height of spring.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:32.800
We've come to the New Forest to see for ourselves the arrival of spring in the British countryside.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:41.800
And to celebrate, we're off on our own little spring break, so I've got everything that we're going to need here in this rucksack, including our tent.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.840
So, just waiting for Julia.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.360
Here she is.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.760
Oh, my word!

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.840
Hello!

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.360
Hello, love.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.440
What are you doing?

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.680
What do you mean?

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.080
I thought we were camping.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.800
Have you not heard of glamping?

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.360
Glamping?

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.760
Glamping is glamorous camping.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.160
It's camping but with some comforts.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.240
Well, I love your flowers.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:01.960
They're nice.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.760
Come on, I'll show you how.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.240
Brilliant.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.120
Do you like the colour?

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.080
It's lovely.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.480
It's called nude.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.840
Some people call it beige.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.800
I've got that much stuff!

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.280
What have you got your frying pan for?

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.600
I thought we'd need the whole lot.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.120
I'll show you how this is done.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.640
You don't need any of that.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.560
Your saggy old tent's going to look very tired.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.320
So, spring has sprung, my lovely.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.200
Hasn't it just?

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.960
Here we are, in the New Forest, absolutely delightful.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:25.560
There's loads of stuff we can do.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.160
We'll have a great spring adventure.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.920
'I'm going lobster potting to catch something luxurious for our lunch.'

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:41.520
'And spring is the start of the sea angling season, 'to see if I can catch an elusive sea bass.'

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:50.840
'We'll be venturing into the Forest to record that spring sensation, the dawn chorus.'

00:01:50.240 --> 00:02:03.600
'And it's the perfect time to do that, 'because this is the weekend of International Dawn Chorus Day 'and spring is the best time of year 'to hear the birds in all their orchestral glory.'

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.200
I do love a good dawn chorus.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.280
But getting up at 5am, it's so hard.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.840
Don't worry, I'll wake you up.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.360
And of course, the others are up to all sorts of bits and pieces as well, aren't they?

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.200
They're all over the country doing spring things.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.680
How do you know when spring has sprung?

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.440
Is the first time you see daffodils like these?

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.120
Or the first time you hear the call of the cuckoo?

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:32.280
With so many different ways out there of telling, I'll be looking when nature decides whether it's spring and why.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:41.600
Spring is all about new beginnings and new opportunities, so I'm out and about looking for ideas to expand my farming business.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:47.480
And at the moment, top of the list is camel racing.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:56.080
Well, these are the kind of spring vegetables you'd have found in shops years ago, but now all this is available.

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.200
We seem to have lost the idea of seasonal food, so what's the impact been?

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.520
I'll be investigating.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:07.080
And I've come to one of the darkest places in the UK for an unrivalled look at the universe.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.640
The New Forest National Park was created in 2005.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:19.360
It covers more than 140,000 acres of woodland, coast and wilderness heaths.

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:30.120
And in keeping with the record numbers of us choosing to holiday in the UK, we're making our way through the Forest to experience our own special version of camping.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.120
Spring really is the perfect time to see the New Forest at its best.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:40.160
You've got all the bluebells, you've got the lime green buds popping through on the trees.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.200
Crocuses, daffodils, blossoms all starting to bud.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.000
Smile and say, spring.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.000
Spring.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:51.920
And new life as well, with loads of lambs around, and if we're lucky, we might be able to spot some of the New Forest foals.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.240
I hope so.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.480
But of course, you know who the expert at spotting spring is?

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:58.800
It's our James.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:07.600
The worst winter in 30 years prompted us all to ask, when will spring begin?

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.960
So did it arrive any later than normal this year?

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.960
And what marks the start of the season?

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:16.280
Well, I'm on a mission to find out.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:27.000
According to the calendar, spring officially starts at the vernal equinox, and that's when the sun crosses the equator for the first time in that year.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:32.520
And that normally happens at the 20th or 21st March, depending on the year.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.040
But when is it to the rest of you?

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.160
I think spring is here when the crocuses are in full bloom.

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.120
Lambs, daffodils.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.400
Daffodils coming out.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:42.960
I always see them on my drive to work and it really represents spring for me.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.680
Easter bunnies.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.400
Lambs in the fields.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.280
Probably the daffodils and the crocuses coming out.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.400
Hopefully a bit of sunshine.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.680
Blossom, sunshine, cutting the grass.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.920
For so many of us, it's the daffodil that's that iconic spring flower.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:04.280
And on this site right here, we've got one of the largest wild populations in the whole country.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:10.560
Andy Bakere, the warden here, has been monitoring this explosion of springtime colour for years.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:16.000
So, Andy, what have you noticed about the flowering of these guys this year?

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:21.320
They're probably about two weeks later than they are normally.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:26.080
So the majority were in bloom round about 30th March, which is round about two weeks later than they would be normally.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.520
Are they your, kind of, key plant for your indicator of spring?

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.000
Yes, absolutely.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:38.560
When you see the trumpet of the daffodil and a bumble bee buzzing away, beavering away inside it, you know, that's a real indication of spring for me.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.920
Two indicators at the same time?

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.320
Absolutely.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.720
Extra bonus points!

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:56.200
So the daffodil, that harbinger of spring, was up to ten days late here in Devon, flowering on or around 30th March.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:00.560
But there are some signs of spring that really aren't that obvious or in-your-face.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:04.560
As I'm in Devon, it would be a crime not to go to the beach.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:12.000
Now, it's been a few years since I've been rock pooling, so today I'm being shown the ropes by some junior rangers.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.440
Apparently, finding a starfish is one sign that spring has begun.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:21.400
While their search goes on, the warden here, James, has already made a discovery.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.320
So what have we got over here?

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:31.640
Well, underneath this rock, I just found a little earlier, some dog whelk eggs that have been laid up there by the whelks.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:35.200
When you see your first eggs, you know that winter's coming to an end?

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.680
Yes, that's right.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:41.200
So it's daylight, it's not temperature, or anything like that that triggers them on to doing it?

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:46.400
No, because the temperature in the pools lags behind the temperature on the land.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.880
What else would you see in this environment that would make you think spring has sprung?

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:57.440
The fish that come into their own at this time of year are things like the really common rock pool fish, which is this blenny here.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:04.920
Never a peacock, a little bit more subdued, but definitely in spring, they're brighter coloured than you'd find in the winter.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:07.080
So why do they change colour in spring?

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.200
It's to attract mates.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.560
Like getting dressed up on a Friday night?

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:12.960
Exactly.

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.400
Putting on your best frock.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.160
Yes.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.160
The junior rangers have got buckets full of sea life now.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:20.280
But are there any starfish?

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.200
We got a big crab.

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:23.240
Yes.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.840
And a velvet something crab.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.240
Uh-huh.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.960
And then the catfish.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:30.760
I found a tiny crab but I don't know where he is.

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.200
Is he hidden underneath?

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.160
There he is.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.560
There he is.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:35.360
That weeny one there.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.160
Look at that!

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.760
And what's in this one?

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.800
That's a devil crab.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.200
That's an enormous one.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.040
Did you pick that up with your hands?

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.760
You're braver than me.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:45.400
I touched that mini crab.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:45.840
It was so cute.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:55.640
Well, plenty of evidence of spring, but no starfish today.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:08:04.800
So, James, I want you to tell me on the calendar, as I hear starfish are really good indicator of spring, when spring's sprung under water?

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:10.560
Well, for me this year, we found loads of starfish around March 12th.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.520
So for me, that was spring under the waves.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.160
So one of the first indicators of spring?

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:22.600
I think in the cold weather, they've gone back out to sea.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.560
Decided it's winter!

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:34.039
Now, I know my study's not exactly scientific, but I'm really fascinated about all the different ways that you can measure when spring has actually sprung.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:42.320
With a really harsh winter, with such late snows, one of the things I'm really interested in is how it's affected things like hibernating mammals.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.520
The Beer Caves in Devon are a vast complex of underground caverns created by centuries of quarrying.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:56.200
They're also where eight species of bat like to hibernate, including the very rare horseshoe bat.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.080
What can these guys tell us about spring?

00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.240
They already seem to be stirring.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:08:59.720
They are stirring slightly.

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:07.000
These are probably the very last of the bats to be hanging on in here.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.720
Very shortly, they'll be going out and starting to feed again.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:13.920
As soon as the brilliant spring weather starts, they'll be off out, foraging.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.960
So as soon as the temperature rises, I'm guessing, in here, that they go out?

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:18.840
Yes.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.000
So in the bat calendar, is it fair to say that spring has already sprung, or half sprung?

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:26.960
I think it's just springing, yes.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:33.240
Certainly if you're out at night now, at dusk, you see the bats flying around.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:39.800
So what date to the bats of Beer suggest to Fiona that spring began this year?

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:46.360
I'm going to put that, if I may, after Easter, because down in Devon here, it became glorious in the middle of that week there.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.840
OK, so spring for bats is later than it is for everyone else.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:54.560
I would say so this year, yes.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:02.640
The sea life in our rock pools and the sleepy bats of Beer each have a different story to tell about the start of spring.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:11.320
But for me, spring has never really sprung until you get one of these trees that just completely enrobes itself in fluorescent pink and white and all shades in between.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:13.720
And it's not just me that thinks it.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:18.680
The National Trust actually gauges spring by when the first magnolias come out.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:27.640
And I happen to know that these magnolias started flowering on the 2nd March.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:31.800
So what does my little calendar of the signs of spring tell me?

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:34.400
Well, I can say in the long term, really not that much.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:48.800
But looking at it, particularly this year after we've had such a long and such a harsh winter, it certainly seems like this year at least, Mother Nature seems that little bit reluctant to cast off her woolly jumper, and like most of us, get out there and have an amazing summer.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:53.440
But when she gets here, it's truly spectacular.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:11:03.160
Well, thankfully now that spring has finally sprung, it's the perfect time for fishing and I fancy lobster for lunch.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:10.720
I'm sure you do, but I'm heading to the beach to try and catch something for the whole Countryfile team, who are joining us for breakfast tomorrow.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:20.000
Spring heralds the start of the shore angling season, so I'm meeting Malcolm Stote and his son Dan.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:25.200
Malcolm fishes for the England team, so if anyone can help me land a big one, it's going to be him.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.600
Malcolm, how are you doing?

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.120
All right?

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.920
Not too bad, thanks.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.000
What are you using here?

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:32.000
Dried worm, this is.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:34.200
It's what we dug here this morning.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.040
We're aiming for what?

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:35.720
What fish?

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:42.360
Anything really, but it's the good thing about the sea angling, you don't really know what you'll catch.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.560
The best thing would be a nice bass.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.160
Is she all ready to go?

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.280
Yes, she's ready to go.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:46.760
You just get that round to there, like that.

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.080
Yes.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:47.480
That's it.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:48.520
Pull it up against the rod.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.560
All right.

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:51.520
And if you put it behind you.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:52.440
Duck, everyone!

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:52.800
There you go.

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.680
Just have to stand and wait now.

00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.320
And that's it?

00:11:57.440 --> 00:11:58.880
That's it.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:02.520
To be fair, I haven't got too long here.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:09.520
What are the chances of catching a sea bass in 40 minutes?

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.440
Possible.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:16.120
Nothing.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.080
'Not a nibble.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:27.160
But thankfully, 'Malcolm has already caught some this morning.'

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:30.080
Is it the case then, Malcolm, with whatever you catch, you can keep?

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.880
Not really.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:36.520
I mean, if you look at this one here, I know this one's way undersize.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:42.000
Our club size is 41 centimetres.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.960
Now, this one measures at 29.

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:45.520
I've got one in the bucket that I would like to show you as well.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:50.200
The thing to watch is you've got some really sharp spines on the back.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:53.560
None of it's poisonous, but they'll make you bleed a bit.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:55.640
Now, this is a big fish, so...

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:56.120
It's a bigger fish, yeah.

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:57.840
So that one's 36, 37, yes?

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:04.440
The Government size, this is 36, which says you can legally keep it.

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:05.720
They are nice to eat.

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:07.640
That is their downfall, to be honest.

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:11.000
But everybody knows that bass is a nice fish to eat.

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:14.120
Right, then, so these three are going to go back in?

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:15.480
Yes.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:19.280
Well, no luck catching something from here for breakfast.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:22.240
But I do have a plan B up my sleeve.

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:28.760
I wonder if Julia's having any more luck.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:35.160
I'm hoping to trump Matt with my fishing skills and I think I've got the slight advantage because I'm actually going out to sea.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.920
Hello, how are you doing?

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:48.880
Not too bad.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:50.480
Good, good.

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:54.440
I hope it's a good day for some lobster.

00:13:53.640 --> 00:13:55.640
I've got lunch to make.

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:07.720
'Now it's spring, the waters of the Solent have warmed up, 'meaning lobsters are on the move and ready to catch.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:13.360
'Stephen Turner's been lobster fishing these waters for nearly 30 years.'

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:16.080
Now, it's been a really cold winter, hasn't it?

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:17.440
So lobsters don't like cold water?

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:17.720
No.

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:18.200
No.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.680
That's why the season's only just starting now.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:23.200
It should have started a month ago, really.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:30.200
Nothing in there.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:31.160
Nothing in that one.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.280
Ah!

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:36.120
Let's check.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.120
Nothing.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:37.400
Nothing.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:41.560
Here we go.

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:44.280
Look at that.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:50.680
So we've got a nice big crab and that looks like a good size lobster.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:53.200
He's going to do it, isn't he?

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:53.880
Look at that.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:57.240
That, ladies and gentlemen, is lunch.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:03.080
There we go.

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:03.360
Number one...

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.680
Number two...

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:10.320
'The freshly baited pots are laid again ready for Stephen to return tomorrow.'

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:15.800
Stephen, it's been a pleasure.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:16.480
Thank you very much.

00:15:15.560 --> 00:15:17.120
Not a problem.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:27.640
With the crustaceans caught, I'm making my way to Michael's mother-in-law's to get them prepared for lunch.

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:29.920
Hello, Julia.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:30.760
Hello.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:31.760
Oh, Julia!

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:32.400
There we go, look.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.680
We've got a lobster.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:34.800
Wonderful.

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:35.400
But a Brucey bonus as well.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.160
Brilliant.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:40.240
What you've got to do now is show me what to do with them.

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.720
Do you want me to do that for you?

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:42.880
Please.

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.840
They go into boiling water...

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.920
..For 12 minutes.

00:15:45.960 --> 00:15:48.600
A lot of people think this is very cruel.

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.880
No, it's not actually because they die instantly.

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:09.280
Whatever Matt's got for lunch, Joan, it's not going to be as delicious as this.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:13.840
And I've also got you some lovely dressed crab.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:14.880
The crab as well.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:15.960
Little extra.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.520
Delicious.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:26.080
Well, I didn't have much luck at the beach.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:32.240
Have a bit more over there(!)

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:38.520
There is plenty here for breakfast because, unlike Julia, I've got the interest of the whole team at heart.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:38.840
There you go.

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:39.760
Get your chops around that.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.720
HE CHUCKLES This is pretty frantic!

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.400
Well, Graham, these are the most fantastic brown trout.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:50.760
Wow.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:53.240
There's a few in there to look at.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:55.120
What age are these, then?

00:16:54.160 --> 00:16:59.680
These fish are about three to three-and-a-half-years-old.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:08.440
So, a bit more food in a few more months and they'll be ready for smoking and serving up with a few scrambled eggs.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:08.960
Do you want to pop them back in?

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.119
Yeah.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.720
They're so athletic!

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:13.160
They're very muscular fish.

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:17.880
They are absolutely beautiful I'd love to try some but I haven't got time to smoke them.

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:21.040
Well, I think we can find some that we made earlier in the fridge.

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:21.520
You can sort us out.

00:17:20.839 --> 00:17:22.720
That's good news.

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:24.640
Can I feed a few more of these fish before I go?

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.440
It's quite an experience, isn't it?

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:34.560
I've worked up an appetite so it's time to eat.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:38.320
What better spot than here deep in the forest?

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.360
The wanderer returns!

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.520
How are you doing?

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:52.480
All right?

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:55.640
Don't say I'm never whip up a meal for you.

00:17:54.680 --> 00:17:55.960
Look at that - your favourite.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:59.400
Absolutely gorgeous.

00:17:58.480 --> 00:17:59.880
Now, that...

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.280
Let me just compare it with what I was thinking of.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:03.600
Cheese sandwiches.

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:03.920
Yeah.

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:06.120
This is about as fresh as you can get.

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:08.320
I caught that lobster about an hour ago.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:08.960
Did you?

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:11.440
Yeah, have a taste.

00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.840
I mean, it's really the definition of seasonal food.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:17.960
So, we've got lobster here, crabs as well.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.680
Little bit of extra.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.000
Yeah.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.360
This was a Brucey bonus.

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.320
I wasn't expecting it.

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:23.360
We got lots, actually, lots of nice big crabs.

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:28.160
Well, John's been to find out what else traditionally should be on our spring plates.

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:35.440
For hundreds of years, we Brits have been importing exotic food from all over the globe.

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:40.320
Bananas, for example, first arrived here in the time of Queen Victoria.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:45.800
But since the end of the Second World War, we've taken it all a step further.

00:18:44.840 --> 00:18:56.560
Now, we're demanding our own traditional fruit and vegetables all year round even though they're only in season naturally for a few weeks every year.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:19:01.720
So, we get our runner beans from Kenya and new potatoes from Egypt.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.400
It's all very convenient.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:04.680
But is it right?

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:13.760
The fact that a lot of things are imported that maybe should not be imported and we should just live on what we get in season and be happy with that.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.680
We've got very good farmland here.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:20.120
All over the countryside and we can actually make our own food if we wanted to.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:22.520
As long as it's on the shelf, I'm happy with it.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:29.160
But we live in a global economy and I'm afraid that there's about eight million of us in London and we all need a bit of food in the evening.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:36.320
40 years ago, if you'd been shopping for fruit and veg in spring, you're choice would have been fairly limited.

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:38.520
Today there's endless choice.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:41.840
It's relatively cheap and as far as food's concerned, there's no such thing as seasons any more.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:47.360
"More's the pity," say some.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:57.240
I'm on my way now to meet Vernon Mascarenhas who supplies seasonal fruit and vegetables to some of the finest hotels and restaurants in London.

00:19:56.400 --> 00:20:05.760
He's convinced that eating seasonally is good for us.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:11.440
It's 8am and Vernon has deliveries to make to some of the most demanding chefs in the capital.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.040
Good morning.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.760
Good morning.

00:20:11.840 --> 00:20:13.640
Nice to see you.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:15.120
What have you got on the van today, then?

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.200
Right, this is what we harvested yesterday.

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:18.840
So, we have some black cabbage here.

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:19.480
We have some Cheltenham beetroot.

00:20:18.840 --> 00:20:24.600
Some Swiss Chard.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:26.120
Red Swiss Chard.

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:28.240
And there we have some purple sprouting broccoli.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:28.960
Can I help you with a box?

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:29.960
Where are we going?

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.640
Just up here to Henry at Racine.

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:33.680
So you believe in seasonal vegetables, then?

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:40.840
Tell him what's coming in season and he has it on his menu the next day.

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:41.400
You serve seasonal stuff in the restaurant?

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:47.040
The whole restaurant's driven by seasonality.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:47.560
It's what brings in the changes to the menu.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:48.960
Here's some black cabbage.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:49.480
Fantastic.

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.880
And some Cheltenham beetroot.

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:51.600
Thanks a lot, Vernon.

00:20:50.680 --> 00:20:51.920
See you.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:52.200
Bye.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:01.520
So, what next, Vernon?

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:05.320
So we've got our fantastic asparagus.

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:07.320
Fresh.

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:07.720
First of the season.

00:21:06.800 --> 00:21:10.560
But isn't it a bit old-fashioned, don't you think?

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:16.000
To be waiting for the seasons when you can get food anytime, anywhere, from any place?

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:17.240
I think it's lovely.

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:17.840
The anticipation.

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:19.400
So, for eight weeks a year we have asparagus.

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:23.040
Then, that's it, we have to wait a whole year for any more.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.920
Nothing like the taste of British asparagus.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:25.040
Absolutely.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:25.480
Here you go, Matt.

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:33.400
And now strawberries.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:37.480
First of the English season.

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:39.920
A lot of people can't wait for a strawberry.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:42.240
They want one at Christmas.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:44.120
They don't taste of anything then.

00:21:43.400 --> 00:21:47.160
These are beautifully delicious English ones.

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:49.040
They'll be on a table at lunchtime.

00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:49.520
Absolutely.

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.240
I know you're in a hurry to get them all delivered.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:55.640
How many London restaurants will get fresh seasonal food today?

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:58.680
We'll do about 200 but we don't just do restaurants.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:02.040
We do shops as well so people can come out and buy it.

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:04.120
So it's not elitist, buying seasonal?

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:04.720
Absolutely not.

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:08.360
But there are times of year when it's pretty boring stuff that's being grown in our fields.

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:12.640
Broccoli, curly kale, things like that.

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:14.080
It is seasonal.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.880
You get celeriac, you have parsnips.

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:17.840
There are over winter apples and pears.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:19.160
You've got quinces.

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:22.200
It's just a different season, that's all.

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:23.280
Hey, what have you got for me today?

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:23.840
Hey, Sanjay.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.080
There's strawberries.

00:22:24.120 --> 00:22:26.400
Beautiful.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:27.040
First of the season.

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:27.280
Great.

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:28.080
Anything else for me?

00:22:27.440 --> 00:22:31.280
I've got some sprouting broccoli, Swiss chard...

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:42.520
Seasonal food champions, like Vernon, question shipping-in supplies like these new potatoes from Egypt which they believe they are using up valuable water resources in an already dry region.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:46.160
For them, it's best to wait until we can grow our own.

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:51.560
Eating seasonal food out of season doesn't necessarily mean shipping it from abroad.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:55.800
These days, we can extend our seasons here in the UK.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:23:06.040
July to October were always the months to eat tomatoes but now vast heated greenhouses mean we can grow them between February and November.

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.640
So, it's time to set out our stall.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:17.320
Is flying in produce worse for the planet than heating greenhouses here in the UK with fossil fuels?

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:24.760
Today, I am going to be marking up my prices not in pounds and pence but in the cost to the environment.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:32.200
Luckily, I don't have to do the maths or the science because someone's already done that, Gareth Edward Jones from Bangor University.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:33.880
Gareth, am I in for a surprise?

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:39.640
You might be, because I think lots of people think that food miles, the distance that food travels, is the biggest cause of the emissions.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:41.440
But lots of other things happen in the food chain that also produce greenhouse gases.

00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:53.840
So, we look at all of these and bring them down into one unit, which we call kilograms of carbon dioxide equivalent, which lets us make comparisons between different food chains.

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:54.360
Let's take an example.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:54.960
What about these tomatoes?

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:59.920
These are British tomatoes and these are imported.

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:01.640
Most British tomatoes will be grown in greenhouses.

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:04.240
The growers put heat in to get the plants to grow well.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:06.400
So, amongst vegetables, tomatoes are quite high.

00:24:05.440 --> 00:24:11.480
They have a measurement of somewhere between two and five, depending where they're grown.

00:24:10.600 --> 00:24:14.040
What about these Spanish ones?

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:17.160
They come straight in lorries to the UK and those lorries emit greenhouse gases.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:23.440
But, actually, surprisingly they emit fewer greenhouse gases, up to about 3.5.

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:24.280
Right.

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:27.800
But it's not that simple, we're not just concerned about greenhouse gases.

00:24:27.560 --> 00:24:33.480
This tomato is about 99.9% water and Spain hasn't got much water.

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:34.080
British apples here.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:40.400
Yeah, British apples, fresh off the tree in October would be about the lowest footprint you could hope for.

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:44.920
They would be about 0.1, 0.15 kilograms of carbon dioxide.

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:51.480
What about this time of year when they're being kept in cold storage?

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.240
You can store British apples for about nine months.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:54.040
At nine months, it would have doubled.

00:24:53.360 --> 00:24:56.280
People like green beans all year round.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.240
These are from Kenya.

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:57.840
What about these?

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:58.440
What's their tally?

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:04.320
A kilogram of beans would have, I'm afraid, an emission of five to nine which is amongst the highest of all vegetables.

00:25:03.400 --> 00:25:09.760
Gareth, you're starting to make me think that to help save the world, I should stop eating fruit and veg.

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:11.160
That's exactly what we shouldn't be saying.

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.400
Here's one thing.

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:21.800
A portion of beans, which, let's say is about that much, by driving your car seven miles, you will have emitted more carbon dioxide than flying that amount of beans into the UK.

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:25.720
So, we need to get these things into perspective.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:35.360
But, in years to come, we may not have to heat greenhouses or fly in as much fruit and vegetables.

00:25:34.400 --> 00:25:39.880
Global warming could make our choice of British produce look very different.

00:25:38.960 --> 00:25:44.800
These walnuts didn't grow in the south of France, or Turkey, or China.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:45.800
They grew here in this orchard, in Kent.

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.760
Could they be a crop of the future, do you think?

00:25:46.960 --> 00:25:48.960
I hope so.

00:25:48.080 --> 00:25:50.280
That's what I believe so that's why I planted them.

00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:52.720
Is it because the temperatures are rising a bit?

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:53.320
That's my bet.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:26:00.480
And with 14 acres of walnut trees reaching maturity, it's a bet he hopes will really come good.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:16.840
If all of those scientists who believe in climate change are proved to be correct, that could open a whole new world here in this country, not just walnuts, but many of what we now consider to be exotic fruits and vegetables could be home-grown, all year round.

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:29.760
For now, though, it's a choice between sticking to genuinely seasonal British food, or pay the environmental price for the alternatives.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.800
That was gorgeous.

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:36.960
Very good.

00:26:36.200 --> 00:26:38.760
I wonder what John's going to bring for breakfast.

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.000
I don't know.

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:42.000
But mushrooms would be good.

00:26:41.200 --> 00:26:43.000
Why?

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:45.400
Well, on the theme of the van.

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:48.120
It's not mushroom coloured, it's nude!

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.200
Thank you.

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:50.720
Right.

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:53.080
What are you doing now?

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:54.440
I'm off to go and see some foals.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.800
Are you?

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.120
Can you do us a favour?

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:56.960
What's that?

00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:58.120
Look after my pan.

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:58.520
OK.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:26:59.440
I'll see you later.

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:00.280
Have fun.

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:01.560
See you in a bit.

00:27:01.280 --> 00:27:07.520
While Matt goes to see some foals, I'm hoping to find some birds.

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:14.040
Later on our trip through the New Forest, we'll be catching up with our breakfast guests.

00:27:13.200 --> 00:27:18.280
Adam's put some camels through their paces but will he back them to make his fortune?

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:24.080
And Katie's been abandoned in a dark forest, with only the stars to guide her.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:39.480
Nearly half the birds in the New Forest National Park are ground-nesting, which means they're particularly vulnerable and susceptible to those with heavy feet.

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:42.640
So, I'd better mind my step.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:48.240
Spring is the start of the breeding season for ground-nesting birds.

00:27:47.560 --> 00:27:56.440
I'm joining Andy Page from the Forestry Commission who monitors them and wants to show me a lapwing nest.

00:27:55.720 --> 00:28:01.360
But they're so well camouflaged, even he struggles to find it.

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:02.760
Just stay where you are.

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:05.520
Don't put your foot anywhere.

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:09.120
You can just see the eggs there.

00:28:08.400 --> 00:28:10.080
Look at that.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.760
We were standing right over them.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:12.840
See them just there?

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:13.560
Very vulnerable.

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:16.360
Yes, very.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:20.080
They've got all the natural predation that can go on here.

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:22.960
Crows and foxes and all that.

00:28:22.280 --> 00:28:30.480
When those chicks hatch, they can run around straight away but they don't fledge for another four or five weeks.

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:31.040
They're sitting pray.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:35.880
They're very easy to be disturbed by dogs or people off the tracks and stuff.

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:36.360
We'll move away.

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:42.080
Andy won't let us stay by the nest for long as the anxious parents are hovering above.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:44.760
While they return to the nest, he wants to show me an even rarer sight.

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:52.160
If I said to you now, there's a ground-nesting bird here with chicks within a few feet of us.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:52.360
Can you see it?

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:52.840
Chicks?

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:53.200
Yes, chicks.

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:53.960
Where?

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:54.400
Oh.

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:54.640
Wow.

00:28:54.120 --> 00:28:55.800
That's extraordinary.

00:28:57.160 --> 00:29:02.680
You're looking at three chicks that are so close to fledging.

00:29:01.720 --> 00:29:14.200
We say to people, come and enjoy the forest but through these really critical spring months, be aware that these birds are nesting on the ground and keep to the main tracks at these important times.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:18.080
It's lovely to see the birds sitting on their nests in spring.

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:24.080
And first thing tomorrow, Matt and I are planning to be up super-early to record the dawn chorus.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:39.520
The New Forest is home to all sorts of wildlife but none is more iconic than the New Forest pony.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:42.400
And at this time of year they're starting to foal.

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:49.360
I'm riding out with Jonathan Garelli, the Head Agister, a kind of warden in the forest.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:52.600
One of his roles is to keep a watchful eye on the animals.

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:57.880
Some of the mares you can see have lost a bit of condition through the winter.

00:29:57.120 --> 00:30:04.040
We've had a very tough winter and the grass is finally starting to come through and they will pick up in condition very quickly.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:06.480
And at what age then would you start foaling?

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:12.680
Probably the mares wouldn't generally get in foal, begin to foal until they're four or five-years-old.

00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:18.000
We do occasionally get a three-year-old and sometimes even a two year-old to have a foal but that is quite rare.

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:20.400
Do you find that they need a bit of help?

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:22.160
No.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:24.400
It's quite marvellous, really.

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:26.920
The ponies need very little interference from us.

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:29.840
They generally go off and foal perfectly all right on their own.

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:33.440
There is always the odd occasion when we do need to intervene but that's very rare.

00:30:32.600 --> 00:30:34.760
We've got one right in front of us there.

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:36.720
White face, white legs.

00:30:35.880 --> 00:30:37.760
How old would this one be?

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:38.320
Coming up to nearly a week old.

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:41.600
Oh!

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:43.920
Have you gone all shy?

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:44.560
Yes, they stay very close to mum at that age.

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:47.840
They don't like to stray too far.

00:30:47.080 --> 00:30:50.280
I've got one here just basking in the sunshine.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:51.120
Hello.

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:51.920
Yes it's a little girl.

00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:55.120
She's only about two or three days old.

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:59.440
Look at her!

00:31:00.760 --> 00:31:06.680
If people are riding through and seeing the animals in the forest, this is about as close as you want to get.

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:08.720
You don't want to disturb them too much.

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:13.160
When they're born, it is all legs and head really, but it will very much change very quickly at the end.

00:31:19.280 --> 00:31:27.240
As well as new life in the countryside, spring also sees the start of the outdoor activity season.

00:31:26.280 --> 00:31:33.040
Increasingly it's an opportunity for inventive farmers to make a bit more extra money off their land.

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:40.600
Adam's been to get some tips on how he could diversify into something off the beaten track.

00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:44.640
I'm a farmer because it's what I always wanted to be.

00:31:43.720 --> 00:31:48.600
It's not just about trying to make a living, it's a way of life.

00:31:47.800 --> 00:32:00.400
My business partner Duncan Andrews and I farm 1,200 acres of arable and have 650 ewes and that gives us a reasonable income.

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:07.400
At the risk of sounding like a whingeing farmer, the problem is with farming and the income it's just so unpredictable.

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:12.840
With the weather, our land can drought out or it can be too wet and wreck the harvest.

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:19.200
Then there is animal diseases like blue tongue and TB that can just wipe out our livestock.

00:32:18.360 --> 00:32:23.200
What we really need to do is to look for incomes outside traditional farming.

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:24.560
That can, you know, boost our whole farming business.

00:32:23.800 --> 00:32:31.640
Diversification is the buzz word that farmers apply to anything slightly off the main farming agenda.

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:36.640
From cheese making to lavender farming for perfumes.

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:46.640
I run a rare breeds farm park alongside the commercial farm, but Dunc and I are always looking for new opportunities, so today we're off to visit a few businesses not far from us.

00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:47.920
Pull!

00:32:47.000 --> 00:32:51.480
Rob Ireland's place is first.

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:54.200
He runs what sounds like a playground for grown-ups.

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:55.880
We do quite a few things here.

00:32:55.080 --> 00:33:00.400
We do the clays, we do quad bikes, we do field archery, we do 4 x 4 off-road driving.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:02.840
We do fly-fishing instruction and fly-fishing.

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:04.280
You name it, we try and do it.

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:05.120
Try and please everybody.

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:07.320
And what about with people coming along shooting?

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:08.840
Presumably a lot of them are novices.

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:10.160
Totally novices.

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:12.760
They've never seen a gun before in their lives.

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:14.320
They get a very full briefing from me.

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:15.240
An introduction to what it's all about.

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:17.240
Safety being paramount.

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:19.320
Dunc's a bit of a master.

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:21.640
Put him through his paces, Rob.

00:33:20.680 --> 00:33:21.800
Let's see.

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:23.920
Do you want to show me how you can do can it?

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:24.560
Why not?

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:30.040
Pull!

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:30.280
Dust.

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:35.280
Pull!

00:33:37.080 --> 00:33:39.600
Right in the middle.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:39.800
Brilliant.

00:33:39.280 --> 00:33:42.200
Nothing wrong with that.

00:33:41.320 --> 00:33:46.000
You can have a job if you want one.

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:46.640
He's mastered that.

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:49.640
Let's go and have a go at something else, shall we?

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:50.560
This is the golf buggy.

00:33:49.640 --> 00:33:53.440
We going for a round of golf?

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:55.360
Yes, if you want but it's a golf buggy with a difference.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:59.880
If you come round this side, guys, I'll quickly explain to you what it's all about.

00:33:59.280 --> 00:34:05.440
One of you is going to sit this side and you'll operate the golf buggy with your right foot.

00:34:05.080 --> 00:34:07.680
Whoever's sitting this side will operate the steering.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:13.159
But it's not quite as straightforward as you think!

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:17.159
'The golf buggy has been modified.

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:24.600
'When I turn left, it goes right and vice-versa.

00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:25.639
'Perfect for a naturally terrible driver like me.'

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:26.360
Yes!

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:26.760
We've got it now!

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:29.040
That's absolutely brilliant, Rob.

00:34:28.239 --> 00:34:30.719
It's just a little bit different, isn't it?

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.600
It's mad!

00:34:32.719 --> 00:34:38.400
How on earth did you get into this game then?

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:41.120
Under pressure from the banks, really.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:45.080
End of the 80s, farming turned a bit nasty for us.

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:51.880
I had a phone call from the bank manager at eight one evening to say that I had to reduce my overdraft.

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:53.199
I said, "By how much?"

00:34:52.280 --> 00:34:53.800
He said, "By half."

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:54.840
I said, "When?"

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:55.840
And he said, "Now."

00:34:54.880 --> 00:35:05.600
So that spurred me on to thinking about other things and we've done the clay shooting and that is my sport and I'd done a little bit of teaching and I thought there's a bit of an opportunity for a business.

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:10.080
For somebody considering setting out on a diversification project, what advice have you got?

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:10.720
You've got to think forward a little bit.

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:16.040
Do a bit of market research - see what's out there and what people are looking for.

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:21.280
If you can come up with something that's a bit quirky then there's a market coming in.

00:35:20.600 --> 00:35:24.920
Are you going to retire and buy that pad in Barbados?

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:27.640
I'd love to but not for a few years yet, I don't think.

00:35:27.200 --> 00:35:37.440
Rob has clearly made a success of his business but I asked the NFU's Phil Bicknell if the bigger picture was as positive.

00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:45.440
We had about 3,000 farm businesses diversify for the first time last year, but we also had 3,000 diversified businesses stop trading.

00:35:44.640 --> 00:35:48.960
But ultimately it adds money to the bottom line of farming.

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:56.360
Round about £300 million last year in England was added to agriculture's value because of diversified farm businesses.

00:35:56.720 --> 00:36:06.400
Beef and arable farmer Ian Branston has found another unusual way of making some extra money from his land.

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:14.320
Hill rolling is a non-spectator extreme activity for which the rolling hills of the Cotswolds are perfect.

00:36:13.520 --> 00:36:15.880
How did this all come about then, Ian?

00:36:16.000 --> 00:36:26.160
It came about by a couple of lads driving along that road and spotting this hill, and then tracking me down at asking if they could roll their balls down it which I was obviously delighted with!

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:28.040
And is it much impact on the field, on the grass?

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:30.680
Absolutely no impact on the grass at all.

00:36:29.720 --> 00:36:33.360
I think it's a thing locally for the community.

00:36:32.680 --> 00:36:40.240
It's great because people come from miles around drinking in the local pubs and use the shops and everything else, so it's fantastic that way.

00:36:39.480 --> 00:36:44.400
And when they are not rolling their balls down the hill our cattle are grazing it.

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:46.280
Is it a money-making venture?

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:49.280
It is probably my most profitable field on the farm.

00:36:48.440 --> 00:36:51.480
By the same token I'm obviously not going to retire on it.

00:36:50.520 --> 00:36:55.160
But it's a very valuable extra income for the farming enterprise.

00:36:54.200 --> 00:36:59.520
This is something we're going to have to try out and I've got a bad back.

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:00.040
Me too.

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:00.440
So, Dunc, you're the man for the job.

00:37:02.120 --> 00:37:03.760
Let's get you in there.

00:37:03.120 --> 00:37:11.000
'Duncan leaps in, quickly followed by a bloke called Pete 'who we persuaded to be Dunc's sphere buddy.'

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:12.080
Maybe we should just let it down!

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:12.320
Woo-hoo!

00:37:17.640 --> 00:37:19.480
Oh, my God.

00:37:19.920 --> 00:37:21.200
Yow!

00:37:20.240 --> 00:37:21.600
Let's go!

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:36.080
How was it, lads?

00:37:39.920 --> 00:37:43.600
Really good.

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:45.840
You don't feel sick?

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:46.600
Not at all.

00:37:45.680 --> 00:37:47.520
Do you want to go again?

00:37:46.680 --> 00:37:49.240
I...maybe....

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:53.360
I've got an overdue library book to get back.

00:37:52.400 --> 00:37:53.560
Otherwise I'd love to!

00:37:53.960 --> 00:37:56.200
What a great sport.

00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:56.680
This is fantastic.

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:57.040
I'm loving it.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:58.120
I'm loving it.

00:37:59.360 --> 00:38:00.480
Whoa!

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:02.000
Wild.

00:38:05.800 --> 00:38:13.920
'Dunc's a little dizzy after that experience so he's left me to go 'and see the final potential money-spinner on my own.'

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:20.160
Joe and Rebecca Fossett run a camel racing business.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:25.120
If you're holding an event, then you can hire these guys to come and entertain the crowds.

00:38:24.280 --> 00:38:28.240
Joe, I've never seen anything like it in the Cotswold hills before.

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:28.800
Unbelievable.

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:31.080
It's certainly different, isn't it?

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:31.720
Amazing!

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:33.000
What's it like working with camels?

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:34.920
I've never stroked a camel before.

00:38:34.160 --> 00:38:35.360
Oh, fantastic.

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:37.200
These camels are absolutely brilliant, they really are.

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:40.120
It depends on the training and handling.

00:38:39.160 --> 00:38:42.080
I've been a long, long time getting these animals like this.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:43.600
It takes a long time, a lot of training.

00:38:42.760 --> 00:38:46.520
They're completely different to anything else you've ever trained.

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:49.240
The couple have six camels.

00:38:48.760 --> 00:38:51.320
Each with very different characters.

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:55.840
Everybody loves Kokoso.

00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:06.040
In fact he's had a play written about him, he gets fan mail and actually we're thinking of him standing as a Member of Parliament because I think he'd get more votes than most of the MPs!

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:16.480
You have to hold a dangerous wild animals licence to own camels, so this isn't a business to be undertaken lightly and come spring these animals need to get back into their training schedule.

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:20.880
After a winter of taking it easy, they need a bit of persuasion.

00:39:19.920 --> 00:39:22.200
Come on!

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:22.840
Come on!

00:39:22.280 --> 00:39:23.960
Look at them go!

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:25.200
THEY LAUGH Come on!

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:28.440
Is there any money in camel racing?

00:39:27.680 --> 00:39:31.680
Well, there's enough to make it worthwhile, I suppose.

00:39:30.840 --> 00:39:33.720
Half of it is the entertainment value, isn't it?

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:35.840
Look at that!

00:39:35.440 --> 00:39:37.920
Oh, Gobi, you always win because you are so greedy!

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:38.200
What a good boy!

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:40.640
'An easy winner.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:45.640
It's been an interesting fact-finding day for Dunc and me.

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:48.560
'The golf buggy could easily be a hit with the kids at our farm park.

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:52.320
'I'd love to own a sphere and try hill rolling.

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:53.320
'But I love animals, so....'

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:59.280
How am I going to break the news to Dunc that I need to buy a couple of racing camels?

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:20.680
This is Roydon Woods on the fringe of the New Forest and it's a peaceful, tranquil and somehow secret spot.

00:40:19.720 --> 00:40:21.960
And now we want to hear from you.

00:40:21.160 --> 00:40:28.160
What do you feel is the perfect place in Britain that sums up all that is unique about our British countryside?

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:32.720
Yes, over the next few weeks we want you to share your secrets with us.

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:42.280
We want your nominations for Secret Britain, for a place that's special to you, that you feel represents the hidden best of our British countryside.

00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:48.720
It might be a remote mountain retreat or the soft falls of a limestone valley.

00:40:48.040 --> 00:40:54.400
A tumbling waterfall or a deserted beach which is special to you and your family.

00:40:55.000 --> 00:41:02.080
A spectacular view or a stunning location for watching wildlife.

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:07.680
We're not looking for the tourist spots that everyone goes to.

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:14.280
We want to hear about those hidden places that are special just to you.

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:16.720
And we want to know not just where, but why?

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:20.760
What is it about your special place that makes it so extraordinary for you?

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:25.720
And which makes it represent the very best of the secret beauty of the British countryside?

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:33.640
Over the summer months, we'll be filming the length and breadth of the UK to show the British Isles at their best.

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:42.240
But we need your nominations to see which of our hidden landscapes will find their way into these very special programmes.

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:48.120
E-mail your thoughts with photos too if you want at...

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:50.520
You'll find all the information you need on the Countryfile website.

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:05.560
Time to head to the campsite and check out our accommodation for the night.

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:09.040
What is that?

00:42:08.240 --> 00:42:10.000
There she is.

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:10.360
Wow.

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:12.120
That is my pod.

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:13.040
You're kidding.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:15.240
This is where you're staying, is it?

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:15.960
Yes.

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:16.360
I can't believe it.

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:20.600
You're going to like this.

00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:22.960
First the van and now this.

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:24.160
Come inside.

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:24.400
Look at the butchers!

00:42:25.880 --> 00:42:26.920
Oh, gosh.

00:42:25.960 --> 00:42:30.880
Most importantly for me, coal burning stove.

00:42:29.920 --> 00:42:32.120
This is incredible, isn't it?

00:42:31.320 --> 00:42:33.600
Got to have music.

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:35.560
Oh, you've got a gramophone!

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:37.280
Nice comfy bed.

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:39.360
Oh, that's so lovely!

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:41.240
John...

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:41.520
Yes?

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:42.720
To watch over me.

00:42:41.960 --> 00:42:43.200
Amazing, isn't it?

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:43.840
Do you like the little stars up there?

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:45.080
Beautiful.

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:46.280
This is massive.

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:47.080
What are you sleeping in, then?

00:42:51.640 --> 00:42:52.800
Right.

00:42:52.600 --> 00:42:57.640
It looks very complicated.

00:42:57.280 --> 00:42:59.040
D'you think that's the door?

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:00.040
OK.

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:05.400
So your poles go straight there like curtain rings?

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:07.160
That's it.

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:09.120
That's the top.

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:09.760
Right.

00:43:08.800 --> 00:43:10.160
I tell you what.

00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:11.640
If you just hang there for a second.

00:43:10.920 --> 00:43:12.760
Just wait there.

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:26.080
You can't open the door.

00:43:25.120 --> 00:43:26.760
Get out.

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:26.960
Get out of my pod!

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:27.840
Out!

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:29.680
It's my pod!

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:30.360
You're a boy!

00:43:30.160 --> 00:43:34.000
You didn't even know what glamping was.

00:43:33.800 --> 00:43:36.960
Oh dear!

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:39.080
Right, I'm not giving you any more help.

00:43:38.160 --> 00:43:39.400
Good luck with that.

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:39.760
'Hmmph!

00:43:41.720 --> 00:43:43.360
That's it from me.'

00:43:42.680 --> 00:43:43.720
Oh dear.

00:43:50.200 --> 00:43:53.520
'Wonder how he's getting on by himself?'

00:43:53.720 --> 00:43:57.280
'Easy peasy.'

00:43:56.320 --> 00:43:59.320
It'll be lovely sleeping out under the stars tonight.

00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:00.720
Yes.

00:43:59.920 --> 00:44:03.760
Mind you, you're not exactly roughing it, are you?

00:44:02.880 --> 00:44:07.560
The important thing is it's a good time of year to star-gaze.

00:44:08.120 --> 00:44:18.280
With temperatures on the rise as we head towards the height of spring and the evenings still dark, this is a great time of year to go star-gazing.

00:44:18.960 --> 00:44:25.000
Sadly, for most of us, when we look up at night, its a blank canvas.

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:31.320
These satellite images demonstrate the problem.

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:37.800
Because we leave so many bulbs burning, our view of the stars is obscured by light pollution.

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:46.120
For 90% of the British population, the best way of seeing the many galaxies out there is by visiting a planetarium like this one in Glasgow.

00:44:46.400 --> 00:44:55.360
They're great places for a basic lesson in how to star spot in the great outdoors, and astronomer Steve Owens is my tutor.

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:55.760
That is Orion, isn't it?

00:44:55.040 --> 00:44:56.520
Those three?

00:44:55.960 --> 00:44:57.280
Am I right?

00:44:56.760 --> 00:44:58.040
That's right.

00:44:57.080 --> 00:44:59.120
This is Orion's belt up here.

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:05.280
Orion is one of the most recognisable constellations in the sky and Orion is a great constellation to learn.

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:19.720
For example, if you go up and to the right of Orion's belt, you go through the V of the horns of Taurus the Bull and you get to this little cluster of stars here called the Seven Sisters.

00:45:18.960 --> 00:45:24.760
In fact, this cluster has thousands of stars in it, but our eyes can only make out seven of them on a good clear night.

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:42.600
Going back to Orion's belt, we can use his belt and his left shoulder to go up across the sky towards these two bright stars, the Gemini twins, and at the moment, beside Gemini, we have very obviously the red dot of Mars.

00:45:41.960 --> 00:45:50.680
Go in the opposite direction, we can find The Plough and these two stars in The Plough are pointers, and they point to the North Star.

00:45:49.760 --> 00:45:54.920
So if you can find the North Star, stand facing it, you'll be facing north.

00:45:54.320 --> 00:45:57.160
Fantastic.

00:45:56.200 --> 00:46:04.520
Now, I have to remember all that information because I'm going to need it later.

00:46:04.400 --> 00:46:09.480
It's amazing to think that we don't really get to see skies like this because of all the light in our cities.

00:46:08.560 --> 00:46:10.280
You're right.

00:46:09.320 --> 00:46:14.200
If you go outside in a city, you'll see 100, maybe 200 stars on a good clear night.

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:18.680
But compare that to the thousands of stars you see in a properly dark sky.

00:46:18.720 --> 00:46:22.400
We were out last night measuring just how bright the skies above Glasgow are.

00:46:21.560 --> 00:46:30.120
The reading we got was about 17 magnitudes per square arcsecond, which is just a measure of how bright the sky is.

00:46:29.680 --> 00:46:32.560
So what would be total darkness?

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:33.360
Total darkness would be about 24 on that scale.

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:37.880
We can go to one of those places now, only a couple of hours' drive out of Glasgow.

00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:38.640
Let's go.

00:46:37.840 --> 00:46:48.840
With the sun sinking below to the Claterringshaws Loch, deep in the heart of Galloway Forest.

00:46:47.880 --> 00:46:58.480
This is the UK's first Dark Sky Park and one of only five in the world and it's a haven for astronomers who want an unobscured view of the universe.

00:46:58.200 --> 00:47:09.280
Galloway isn't the most remote place in the UK, but it's the perfect spot for a Dark Skies Park because it's close enough to Scotland's big cities to attract lots of visitors.

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:13.080
As night falls, I'm one astro-tourist getting quite excited.

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:17.440
Well, it's definitely past dusk now and it is well and truly night time.

00:47:16.520 --> 00:47:20.880
If you look up into the sky, it is spectacular.

00:47:20.440 --> 00:47:26.360
It's almost like there's too many stars up there in the sky.

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:26.720
It's unbelievable.

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:35.080
What we'll do is measure just how dark it is here and we'll compare that to the readings that Steve took in Glasgow yesterday.

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:38.280
To do that, we need to turn off these lights.

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:41.680
And use an infra-red camera.

00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:43.160
Yesterday in Glasgow, it read about 17.

00:47:42.240 --> 00:47:46.120
And here in the park, it's reading over 20.

00:47:45.320 --> 00:47:47.360
And that's what you'd expect?

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:47.840
Absolutely.

00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:54.560
In fact you can go to darker parts of the park and the very dark core of the park, that might read as dark as 21, 22.

00:47:54.040 --> 00:48:00.080
'So, that's where we're going and I'm going to use the stars to navigate.'

00:47:59.640 --> 00:48:02.440
Can you find north?

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:05.720
Well, I have to swing over this way.

00:48:04.880 --> 00:48:07.200
There's The Plough.

00:48:06.240 --> 00:48:09.320
It looks like it's in a different position...

00:48:08.360 --> 00:48:11.680
And there's the Pole Star.

00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:12.360
That's right.

00:48:11.520 --> 00:48:14.800
'Fantastic.

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:17.000
I'm really getting into this astro-tourism.'

00:48:16.040 --> 00:48:23.560
I thought I was going to be gardening and bird-watching in later life, but I think I might go for astronomy first.

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:24.560
A little bit cold.

00:48:23.600 --> 00:48:25.600
'Steve is right.

00:48:24.640 --> 00:48:26.520
It's absolutely freezing and completely, totally dark.'

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:38.120
'A few hundred yards further into the forest 'and the darkness claims its first victim.'

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:39.240
Oops!

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:48.880
S 'After recovering my composure and navigating my way further into the forest, a shadowy figure appears.'

00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:50.320
What are we looking at?

00:48:49.360 --> 00:48:54.000
Can you see the blur from the sky there?

00:48:53.120 --> 00:48:55.560
Oh, look at that.

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:56.040
Yes, I can.

00:48:55.600 --> 00:48:56.640
So you know what that is?

00:48:55.800 --> 00:48:59.320
That's the Seven Sisters?

00:48:58.440 --> 00:49:00.720
That's the Seven Sisters.

00:49:00.120 --> 00:49:09.960
The projection of the night sky looked great in the planetarium but the real thing is just breathtaking.

00:49:09.960 --> 00:49:15.960
How would you like to see the first signs of spring?

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:17.000
In the sky.

00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:18.360
We can tell.

00:49:17.520 --> 00:49:18.720
Well, you tell me.

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:21.600
Where is The Plough?

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:21.880
The Plough's there.

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:32.280
If you follow the droop down from the arm, that star over on the horizon there, the bright one that twinkles a little bit more, OK, is the first star that shows the beginning of spring arriving.

00:49:31.400 --> 00:49:37.800
That's Arcturus and it's one of the first signs that spring's on the way.

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:41.440
Can you tell the seasons throughout the year by looking at the sky at night?

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:41.680
Absolutely.

00:49:40.760 --> 00:49:45.360
The sky changes over the course of the seasons.

00:49:44.480 --> 00:49:49.680
In the summer you get a group of stars called the Summer Triangle, which you can't see in winter.

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:55.640
And equally, Orion is out up at the moment but you can't see Orion in the summer.

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:56.200
It's a winter constellation.

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:56.640
You can't see it in the summer?

00:49:55.680 --> 00:49:56.800
No.

00:49:55.880 --> 00:49:58.440
'And this is what's on show tonight in Galloway Forest Park.'

00:50:00.400 --> 00:50:05.720
How important is Dark Sky Park status to this area?

00:50:04.920 --> 00:50:08.440
Everyone's enjoying the darkness around here.

00:50:07.520 --> 00:50:11.760
It brings in people in the winter that wouldn't normally come.

00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:13.600
They're staying here.

00:50:12.680 --> 00:50:22.280
But the great thing is, they're all learning how good it is to have true darkness and how important it is to have true darkness for us and the wildlife and everything else around us.

00:50:22.800 --> 00:50:25.200
And it really is a revelation.

00:50:24.320 --> 00:50:29.560
As a city girl, it feels like light years since I've seen a sky like this.

00:50:28.800 --> 00:50:40.560
Within just a few minutes of sky-gazing, I've seen The Plough and Mars, and a man-made treat, the international space station orbiting through the night sky, passing in front of Orion.

00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:48.720
It's amazing to think that not so long ago, anyone could step outside their house at night and see skies like this.

00:50:47.760 --> 00:50:55.840
But for most of us, there's somewhere dark enough to see the night sky just a few hours' drive away.

00:50:54.880 --> 00:50:58.520
So I'd highly recommend a night trip to the countryside this spring.

00:51:04.960 --> 00:51:06.760
Hiya.

00:51:07.880 --> 00:51:10.720
Hi.

00:51:10.600 --> 00:51:16.200
I understand it's going to be pretty cold tonight, so if you do happen to leave the keys to the camper under the plant pot, then I wouldn't object.

00:51:15.280 --> 00:51:17.240
OK.

00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:17.560
Right.

00:51:16.600 --> 00:51:18.120
You sleep well.

00:51:17.200 --> 00:51:20.120
See you in the morning.

00:51:19.160 --> 00:51:20.480
Night.

00:51:55.920 --> 00:52:08.360
It's just gone 5am, and since it's the weekend of International Dawn Chorus Day, it seems like the right time to record the birds here in one of the best places in the UK to listen to them.

00:52:07.560 --> 00:52:16.400
We're up at this crazy hour, we're going to give the others, who are making their way over, the experience of waking up in the woodlands.

00:52:15.640 --> 00:52:17.480
We certainly are.

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:19.840
So we'll record the dawn chorus.

00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:23.400
Our sound man will help us do that with all sorts of special equipment.

00:52:22.560 --> 00:52:25.480
Morning, Rob.

00:52:24.640 --> 00:52:25.880
Morning.

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:31.600
We head off into the woods near the camp site to meet bird expert Nigel Matthews.

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:33.600
It sounds fantastic, doesn't it, already?

00:52:34.080 --> 00:52:38.000
Rob positions the microphones as if he was recording an orchestra.

00:52:37.040 --> 00:52:38.200
But why are they making all this noise?

00:52:37.240 --> 00:52:43.080
It's like you singing, this is where you live and you don't want me to come...

00:52:42.280 --> 00:52:45.040
Territorial song?

00:52:44.080 --> 00:52:45.280
Territorial.

00:52:44.320 --> 00:52:52.520
A bit of display as well, with the males saying, I'm a good songster.

00:52:51.800 --> 00:52:54.040
Ready?

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:54.160
Yes.

00:53:31.320 --> 00:53:34.040
Lovely.

00:53:33.080 --> 00:53:37.160
Right, now, talk us through some of the sounds.

00:53:37.080 --> 00:53:38.280
OK.

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:42.200
So it's ju-dit, ju-dit, ju-dit...

00:53:41.360 --> 00:53:45.120
It's a song thrush.

00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:45.360
And then we've got wren.

00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:51.400
There's lots of trills and churs following each other.

00:53:50.440 --> 00:53:56.680
We've had blackbirds, we've had robin, there's a willow warbler.

00:53:56.120 --> 00:54:05.440
When they're all singing together it is very much like an orchestra, actually and you can't pick out individual instruments.

00:54:04.960 --> 00:54:06.000
Is it particularly strong at this time of year?

00:54:06.440 --> 00:54:09.320
Yes, April, May, it's just a fantastic time of year.

00:54:08.480 --> 00:54:10.200
Really, really good.

00:54:09.440 --> 00:54:18.800
And if you go to the Countryfile website, you can listen again to the New Forest dawn chorus in all its springtime glory.

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:22.160
Hello!

00:54:21.360 --> 00:54:22.560
Anybody here?

00:54:21.760 --> 00:54:25.640
I've seen a door, a chimney and topiary plants outside.

00:54:26.120 --> 00:54:27.840
It's good, isn't it?

00:54:26.960 --> 00:54:30.360
Guess which tent is Julia's!

00:54:29.480 --> 00:54:33.520
'While we're enjoying the peace of the wood, there are intruders in the campsite.'

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:37.400
'But at least they've brought edible gifts.

00:54:37.240 --> 00:54:39.560
I could murder a sausage!'

00:54:38.800 --> 00:54:40.880
Are you a bit of a chef, John?

00:54:40.160 --> 00:54:42.680
Yes, brilliant, I am.

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:42.920
Really?

00:54:42.120 --> 00:54:43.200
No.

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:44.840
THEY LAUGH Invaders in our camp.

00:54:44.200 --> 00:54:46.640
Hello, hello.

00:54:45.800 --> 00:54:48.520
How did you know where we were?

00:54:49.200 --> 00:54:53.560
'I've got a contribution from my travels too.'

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:57.200
What's he got there?

00:54:56.520 --> 00:54:58.400
How about this for a little starter?

00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:01.880
Smoked brown trout.

00:55:01.120 --> 00:55:03.040
From just around the corner.

00:55:02.160 --> 00:55:03.720
Eggs, Matt?

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:04.400
Yeah.

00:55:03.520 --> 00:55:05.680
Oh, lovely.

00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:09.280
Honey as well.

00:55:13.120 --> 00:55:23.240
'As we start to tuck in, I've got an extra accompaniment for the meal, 'this morning's dawn chorus.'

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:24.840
So, do you know what they all are?

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:25.720
Can you tell us?

00:55:25.000 --> 00:55:28.360
That's a song thrush.

00:55:27.560 --> 00:55:29.800
Very good.

00:55:28.880 --> 00:55:34.080
Oh, and that's a spotted Vauxhall Astra you can hear in the background.

00:55:33.120 --> 00:55:34.920
That's bird-watching.

00:55:34.080 --> 00:55:36.120
Lovely to listen to birdsong all day, but we've got a job to do.

00:55:36.080 --> 00:55:37.440
We certainly have.

00:55:37.360 --> 00:55:41.680
One of the New Forest Rangers has asked us if we'll give him a hand shifting some of his cattle to some fresh grass.

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:50.000
Julia and I are on a mission.

00:55:49.120 --> 00:55:54.920
Local warden Michael Boxall and his son Martin are in charge.

00:55:54.040 --> 00:55:55.160
Hi, Martin.

00:55:54.520 --> 00:55:56.360
How are you doing?

00:55:55.480 --> 00:55:56.680
Hello.

00:55:55.840 --> 00:55:57.800
What's the plan?

00:55:56.960 --> 00:56:01.360
We're going to move the cattle away from their wintering grounds.

00:56:00.560 --> 00:56:06.480
They've been up here since December and we're going to start them on the round of the reserve.

00:56:05.800 --> 00:56:09.360
They graze various parts through the year.

00:56:08.400 --> 00:56:10.560
Throughout all the seasons, yes?

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:12.280
Yes.

00:56:11.440 --> 00:56:12.960
We'll take them through the wood down to a meadow by the river.

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:20.360
Seven very pregnant cows about to give birth any day, and half a mile of woodland between them and the bottom pasture.

00:56:19.400 --> 00:56:21.760
Easy.

00:56:20.960 --> 00:56:22.520
Come on.

00:56:21.560 --> 00:56:22.760
Come on, girls.

00:56:22.720 --> 00:56:26.560
Shall we go for a little stroll?

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:27.320
Let's get up behind them.

00:56:26.440 --> 00:56:30.480
'The cows were a bit reluctant to follow us, but John's on the case.'

00:56:30.360 --> 00:56:33.680
Come on, John, get your arms out.

00:56:32.840 --> 00:56:34.240
That red coat.

00:56:33.280 --> 00:56:34.520
Come on.

00:56:33.760 --> 00:56:35.640
Put a bit of pace into it.

00:56:34.720 --> 00:56:37.920
'And it seems the cows aren't the only reluctant ones.'

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:39.960
Come on, Katie, come on.

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:40.120
Come on.

00:56:39.160 --> 00:56:40.680
Galloping.

00:56:40.200 --> 00:56:41.480
Oh, here we go.

00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:41.800
There we go.

00:56:40.920 --> 00:56:42.800
Now they're moving.

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:44.560
Who's bringing up the rear?

00:56:43.840 --> 00:56:49.960
Oh, steady, steady.

00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:50.280
There they go.

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:50.920
Come on.

00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:51.920
Come on, girls.

00:56:51.080 --> 00:56:53.120
Come on, girls, here we go.

00:56:52.200 --> 00:56:54.880
It looks like the cows know where they're going.

00:56:54.000 --> 00:56:55.360
Come on, girls.

00:56:54.400 --> 00:56:55.800
Come on.

00:56:56.280 --> 00:56:58.360
Come on.

00:56:58.480 --> 00:57:02.160
Steady down.

00:57:01.320 --> 00:57:04.440
If you hear a big rumbling in a minute, just step to the side and let them go by.

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:05.600
Yes.

00:57:07.080 --> 00:57:09.720
Giddy up, there.

00:57:10.240 --> 00:57:12.520
I heard the rumble.

00:57:11.720 --> 00:57:15.840
Steady!

00:57:15.360 --> 00:57:18.480
Steady, there.

00:57:19.520 --> 00:57:21.880
Are we taking this channel here, up to the left?

00:57:20.920 --> 00:57:21.960
Going up there, yes.

00:57:25.360 --> 00:57:27.400
Come on, girls.

00:57:26.440 --> 00:57:27.840
Hang a left.

00:57:27.960 --> 00:57:29.760
'It's all going swimmingly until the cows get their lefts and their rights mixed up 'and go completely off piste.

00:57:33.640 --> 00:57:34.680
'Closely followed by Matt.'

00:57:34.760 --> 00:57:36.320
It looks like they've run off, John.

00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:39.440
A stampede over the hill.

00:57:39.000 --> 00:57:43.000
Not our fault, though, is it?

00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:43.440
I don't think so.

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:44.240
Just let them settle a little bit there, Jim.

00:57:48.600 --> 00:57:49.640
They have gone completely the wrong way.

00:57:49.040 --> 00:57:51.440
I think they're a bit star-struck, to be honest.

00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:52.400
Matt Baker.

00:57:54.720 --> 00:57:58.840
'They're soon back on track and heading towards their summer grazing.'

00:57:58.840 --> 00:58:00.280
Come on, girls.

00:57:59.400 --> 00:58:03.160
Steady, steady, there you go.

00:58:06.880 --> 00:58:08.200
Ah, there we go.

00:58:07.320 --> 00:58:10.280
They look like happy cows now, Michael.

00:58:09.440 --> 00:58:10.600
Yes.

00:58:09.680 --> 00:58:11.600
They're very happy cows, Julia.

00:58:11.080 --> 00:58:12.960
See you later, Michael.

00:58:12.040 --> 00:58:13.840
Thank you.

00:58:12.960 --> 00:58:16.160
Enjoy the grass and happy calving!

00:58:16.320 --> 00:58:18.880
A good job well done, don't you think?

00:58:17.920 --> 00:58:22.240
That is it from the New Forest for our spring special.

00:58:21.520 --> 00:58:23.200
We'll be back next week, business as usual on Sunday.

00:58:24.040 --> 00:58:25.200
We'll see you then.

00:58:26.880 --> 00:58:28.920
Bye bye.
